For low-frequency small-signal analysis, \(V_{DD}\) and the bias voltages are ac grounds. The top transistor \(M_2\) presents \(r_{o2}\) at node \(X\), and \(M_1\) is degenerated by that resistance. Hence

\[
\boxed{R_{\rm out}
= r_{o1}+r_{o2}+(g_{m1}+g_{mb1})r_{o1}r_{o2}
\approx (g_{m1}+g_{mb1})r_{o1}r_{o2}}
\]

If body effect is neglected, set \(g_{mb1}=0\):

\[
\boxed{R_{\rm out}\approx g_{m1}r_{o1}r_{o2}
= (g_{m1}r_{o1})r_{o2}}
\]

since usually \(g_m r_o\gg 1\). Here \(M_1\) is the lower transistor (nearest \(R_{\rm out}\)); if your notation numbers the cascode device as \(M_2\), interchange the subscripts \(1\leftrightarrow2\).

Assuming low-frequency small-signal operation, the transistors biased in saturation, and neglecting channel-length modulation and body effect (\(r_o\to\infty\)), the circuit is a **shunt-shunt negative-feedback transimpedance amplifier**:

- \(M_1\) is a common-gate input transistor whose gate is at ac ground through the bias source \(V_b\).
- \(M_2\) is a common-source inverting gain stage.
- \(R_F\) feeds the output voltage back to the input node, forming negative feedback.

Let
\[
v_s=\text{small-signal voltage at the source of }M_1,\qquad
v_o=V_{\rm out}\text{ (small-signal)}
\]
and let \(I_{\rm in}\) be positive in the direction of the arrow in the figure.

### Small-signal equations
\[
v_x=g_{m1}R_{D1}v_s
\]
\[
\left(\frac1{R_{D2}}+\frac1{R_F}\right)v_o
+g_{m2}v_x-\frac{v_s}{R_F}=0
\]
\[
I_{\rm in}=\left(g_{m1}+\frac1{R_F}\right)v_s-\frac{v_o}{R_F}
\]

Solving gives the closed-loop transresistance:
\[
\boxed{
Z_T=\frac{V_{\rm out}}{I_{\rm in}}
=
\frac{
R_{D2}\left(1-g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_F\right)
}{
1+g_{m1}(R_F+R_{D2})
+g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}
}
}
\]
For the usual case of large loop gain,
\[
g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}\gg 1+g_{m1}(R_F+R_{D2}),
\]
this reduces to
\[
\boxed{V_{\rm out}\approx -I_{\rm in}R_F}
\]
so the transresistance is approximately \(-R_F\). The negative sign is because an increasing input current raises \(v_x\), turning on \(M_2\) more and pulling \(V_{\rm out}\) down. If \(I_{\rm in}\) is defined in the opposite direction, the sign reverses.

The small-signal input resistance seen by the current source is
\[
\boxed{
R_{\rm in}=\frac{v_s}{I_{\rm in}}
=
\frac{
R_F+R_{D2}
}{
1+g_{m1}(R_F+R_{D2})
+g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}
}
}
\]
Thus negative feedback lowers the input resistance; without feedback,
\[
R_F\to\infty \quad\Rightarrow\quad R_{\rm in}\to \frac1{g_{m1}}.
\]

The output resistance, with the input current source opened, is
\[
\boxed{
R_{\rm out}
=
\left[
\frac1{R_{D2}}
+
\frac{g_{m1}(1+g_{m2}R_{D1})}
{1+g_{m1}R_F}
\right]^{-1}
}
\]
so feedback also lowers the output resistance; with \(R_F\to\infty\), \(R_{\rm out}\to R_{D2}\).

The voltage gain from the input node to the output node is
\[
\boxed{
A_v=\frac{v_o}{v_s}
=
\frac{
R_{D2}\left(1-g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_F\right)
}{
R_{D2}+R_F
}
}
\]
and without feedback (\(R_F\to\infty\)),
\[
A_v\to -g_{m1}g_{m2}R_{D1}R_{D2}.
\]

### Bias conditions
For saturation (assuming \(M_2\)'s source is ground),
\[
M_1:\quad V_X\ge V_b-V_{TH1},
\]
\[
M_2:\quad V_{\rm out}\ge V_X-V_{TH2},
\]
together with \(V_b-V_S>V_{TH1}\), where \(V_S\) is the dc voltage at the input node. \(R_{D1}\), \(R_{D2}\), \(V_b\), and \(R_F\) must be chosen to satisfy these inequalities.

### Main conclusions
- The circuit converts input current to output voltage; ideally \(V_{\rm out}\approx -I_{\rm in}R_F\).
- \(M_1\) provides a low-impedance current-input node.
- \(M_2\) supplies inverting gain, allowing large loop gain.
- \(R_F\) sets the transresistance, stabilizes the dc operating point, lowers input/output resistance, and increases usable bandwidth, subject to stability/compensation requirements.

If the bulk of \(M_1\) is grounded, replace \(g_{m1}\) in the formulas by \(g_{m1}+g_{mb1}\). Finite \(r_{o1},r_{o2}\) can be included by adding their conductances in the small-signal nodal equations; the approximations above remain valid when the loop gain is large.

Assume each inverter is a CMOS inverter biased in its small-signal (linear) region. By symmetry, a small test voltage \(v_x\) applied at the common supply node shifts all three internal inverter nodes by the same small-signal voltage. Hence, for each inverter the small-signal input and output voltages are effectively equal, so its PMOS and NMOS devices look like two diode-connected MOSFETs in series between the supply node and ground.

Using \(g_{mN},g_{mP}\) for the device transconductances and \(g_{dsN},g_{dsP}\) for their output conductances, one inverter branch has resistance
\[
R_{\rm branch}
=
\frac{1}{g_{mP}+g_{dsP}}
+
\frac{1}{g_{mN}+g_{dsN}}.
\]
The three inverters are in parallel at the supply node, so
\[
\boxed{
R_X
=
\frac{1}{3}\left[
\frac{1}{g_{mP}+g_{dsP}}
+
\frac{1}{g_{mN}+g_{dsN}}
\right]
=
\frac{g_{mN}+g_{mP}+g_{dsN}+g_{dsP}}
{3(g_{mP}+g_{dsP})(g_{mN}+g_{dsN})}
}
\]
This is a positive resistance for a symmetric ring linearized about its quiescent point.

For the usual symmetric case \(g_{mN}=g_{mP}=g_m\) and
\(g_{dsN}=g_{dsP}=g_{ds}\),
\[
\boxed{
R_X=\frac{2}{3(g_m+g_{ds})}
\approx \frac{2}{3g_m}
\quad (g_{ds}\ll g_m)
}
\]
If the inverter transconductance is instead quoted as the total transconductance
\(G_m=g_{mN}+g_{mP}\), then for symmetric devices the approximate result is
\[
\boxed{R_X\approx \frac{4}{3G_m}}.
\]

For ideal logic inverters with no dc conduction at the operating point, the small-signal resistance would instead be infinite; the finite result above applies when both devices are on, as in the linearized mid-rail operating point of a CMOS ring oscillator.

Increasing the widths of **M5 and M6 normally degrades speed** (increases the comparator delay), unless the devices were initially undersized.

M5/M6 are the cross-coupled PMOS pull-up/latch devices. During the decision phase, the output nodes \(X\) and \(Y\) are precharged high, and one of them must be pulled low by the input pair and the NMOS cross-coupled pair M3/M4. Making M5/M6 wider:

- adds gate/drain/junction capacitance at \(X\) and \(Y\), so the critical node discharges more slowly;
- increases the PMOS pull-up strength, which can oppose the desired common-mode/differential change while the latch is resolving;
- gives stronger positive feedback only after a differential has already developed, so it does not help the initial decision time.

There is an optimum: too-small M5/M6 gives weak regeneration; too-large M5/M6 gives excessive capacitance/contention. For a normally sized high-speed comparator, widening them further usually **reduces speed**, and also increases power and kickback.

Neglecting channel-length modulation and transistor capacitances, and assuming the drain of \(M_2\) is tied to node \(X\) (diode-connected, as drawn), define

\[
g_{\rm eff}=\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}} .
\]

The open-loop small-signal voltage gain from \(P\) to \(V_{\rm out}\) is

\[
A_v=\left.\frac{v_{\rm out}}{v_P}\right|_{R_F\to\infty}
=
-\frac{g_{m1}R_{D1}\,g_{\rm eff}R_{D2}}{1+g_{\rm eff}R_{D1}} .
\]

With the shunt feedback resistor \(R_F\) and output resistance \(R_o\simeq R_{D2}\), the low-frequency active input resistance is

\[
\boxed{
R_{\rm in}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}{1- A_v}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+\dfrac{g_{m1}R_{D1}g_{\rm eff}R_{D2}}{1+g_{\rm eff}R_{D1}}}
}
\]

or, equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
R_{\rm in}
=
(R_F+R_{D2})\,
\frac{1+g_{\rm eff}R_{D1}}
{1+g_{\rm eff}R_{D1}+g_{m1}R_{D1}g_{\rm eff}R_{D2}}
}
\]

with
\[
g_{\rm eff}=\frac{g_{m2}g_{m3}}{g_{m2}+g_{m3}}.
\]

The total input impedance, including the input capacitor \(C_{\rm in}\), is

\[
\boxed{
Z_{\rm in}(s)
=
R_{\rm in}\parallel \frac{1}{sC_{\rm in}}
=
\left(
sC_{\rm in}
+
\frac{1+|A_v|}{R_F+R_{D2}}
\right)^{-1},
\qquad s=j\omega .
}
\]

For \(g_{m2}=g_{m3}=g_m\), this becomes

\[
|A_v|
=
\frac{g_{m1}R_{D1}\,g_mR_{D2}}
{2+g_mR_{D1}},
\qquad
R_{\rm in}
=
\frac{R_F+R_{D2}}
{1+\dfrac{g_{m1}R_{D1}g_mR_{D2}}{2+g_mR_{D1}}}.
\]

In the usual high-gain approximation \(g_mR_{D1}\gg2\) and \(g_{m1}R_{D2}\gg1\),

\[
\boxed{
R_{\rm in}\approx \frac{R_F}{1+g_{m1}R_{D2}},
\qquad
Z_{\rm in}\approx
\left(
sC_{\rm in}
+
\frac{1+g_{m1}R_{D2}}{R_F}
\right)^{-1}.
}
\]

If the question asks only for the input resistance looking into the amplifier, use the \(R_{\rm in}\) expression above; include \(C_{\rm in}\) only when the total input impedance at the source node is required.
